      "text": "To find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we start by recalling the formula for the projection matrix onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we need to check if the projection matrix is invertible. A matrix is invertible if and only if its determinant is non-zero. The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\det\\left( \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{13} \\right)^2 \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{169} (9 \\cdot 4 - 6 \\cdot 6) = \\frac{1}{169} (36 - 36) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is zero, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is not invertible. Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Does not exist}}\n\\]",
